So these rules can help us in proving whether a language is context-free or not, or a language is regular or not, etc.

231
00:46:58,036 --> 00:47:01,456
Now, another application.

232
00:47:04,256 --> 00:47:09,135
Now, this time we will prove that L is not context-free.

233
00:47:10,135 --> 00:47:11,336
And what is L?

234
00:47:12,536 --> 00:47:20,836
We have a W where number of A's, number of B's, and number of C's are equal.

235
00:47:23,860 --> 00:47:25,739
And remember, I said that.

236
00:47:26,019 --> 00:47:37,119
So if you have two things, two numbers, related numbers, context-free languages do that.

237
00:47:39,519 --> 00:47:42,360
Context-free grammars can produce it.

238
00:47:42,579 --> 00:47:45,440
Context-free pushdown automata can accept it.

239
00:47:47,440 --> 00:47:51,100
But if you have three related values,

240
00:47:52,019 --> 00:47:58,340
Context-free grammars cannot produce it, pushdown automata cannot accept it.

241
00:47:58,340 --> 00:48:09,060
So that kind of language, this kind of language where three values are related is not context-free in general.

242
00:48:11,119 --> 00:48:13,300
But how do we prove this?

243
00:48:23,443 --> 00:48:25,355
Well, um...

244
00:48:28,724 --> 00:48:29,684
Let's see.

245
00:48:34,387 --> 00:48:38,087
We have our L already. It's this guy.

246
00:48:40,587 --> 00:48:52,007
And now we introduce a regular language. This guy. A to the star, B to the star, T to the star. This is regular.

247
00:48:55,068 --> 00:48:59,407
Now, if L was context-free,

248
00:49:01,780 --> 00:49:12,380
Its intersection with this regular language would give this language and it would be context-free.

249
00:49:14,880 --> 00:49:23,140
But we already know that this is not context-free. A to the N, B to the N, C to the N is not context-free. We know it.

250
00:49:24,820 --> 00:49:27,619
We proved it in our previous slides.

251
00:49:29,235 --> 00:49:35,055
So, this is like a proof by contradiction, right?

252
00:49:36,555 --> 00:49:45,935
If L was context-free, A to the N, B to the N, C to the N would be context-free, but it is not.

253
00:49:46,635 --> 00:49:50,036
So, L should not be context-free, right?

254
00:49:51,155 --> 00:49:54,076
We are using a proof by contradiction here.

255
00:50:14,068 --> 00:50:23,128
So, this was the end for this slide.

256
00:50:23,128 --> 00:50:37,128
So, as a summary, knowing these closure properties, whether a family of...

257
00:50:37,748 --> 00:50:48,128
Languages, like context-free languages or regular languages, are closed under various operations or not.

258
00:50:49,128 --> 00:51:02,948
Under various operations, helps us improving whether a language is context-free or regular or not.

